\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS- \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  could work if policies match-IMF. \b0\par\par\uc2 512 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 1999\par 19:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Sept 22 (Reuters) - A stronger yen could hurt Japan's economy as it recovers from recession and the time may be right for countries to try jointly to curb the abrupt yen gains, the International Monetary Fund said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  IMF chief economist Michael Mussa, speaking at a news conference launching the fund's World Economic Outlook, said Japan was right to be concerned about the level of the yen, which has been rising against the dollar on optimism that Japan's economy was finally recovering from its painful slump. \par \par \uc2  "There are risks that the recovery could be knocked off course if we now see a substantial appreciation of the yen," Mussa said. He added: "The yen is today about where it should be. A further significant appreciation of the yen would represent overshooting in the medium term." \par \par \uc2  The yen is trading near 104 to the dollar, up sharply from its 1999 low of around 124.70. Since Japan released more strong growth data on Sept. 9, the yen has staged a smart rally from 111.20 to a 3-1/2 year high near 103.20, hit last week. \par \par \uc2  Mussa, launching into the debate on whether central banks should now \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly by selling yen to drive the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower, said concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could work if it was backed by the right monetary policies. \par \par \uc2  "This is a situation where it makes sense, provided credible means are available," he said. "If \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not backed by monetary policy, we tend to believe that its effect will not be very large." \par \par \uc2  "Circumstances now prevailing in Japan and in the global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are ones in which the exchange rate is a concern of monetary policy," Mussa added. \par \par \uc2  Mussa said he was sure that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates would be discussed at this weekend's upcoming meeting of the Group of Seven industrialised countries. \par \par \uc2  Japan, which has repeatedly sold yen to try to drive the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower, is expected to ask its G7 partners to abandon their hands-off approach on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and back its efforts. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , anxious that Japan do all it can to stimulate domestic demand, has so far been wary. \par \par \uc2  Mussa said it would not be appropriate to sterilise the effects of any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action completely. \par \par \uc2  Central banks generally "sterilize" \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling securities to mop up the money they have pumped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, thereby neutralising the effect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on money supply growth. \par \par \uc2  Mussa said the level of the dollar was less significant to the U.S. economy than the yen level was to the Japanese one, but a weaker dollar would be needed in the medium term to reduce the wide U.S. current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  "We believe that in the medium term the dollar does need to correct downward if we are to adjust the U.S. current account deficit down," he said. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the U.S. government reported at a record $25.18 billion trade gap for July. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9m03bc2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngb