\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Japan politicians vent frustration over high yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Reed Stevenson \par \uc2 911 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 1999\par 07:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 22 (Reuters) - Senior Japanese politicians on Wednesday kept up a chorus of concern over the strong yen and maintained pressure on the Bank of Japan to add a lending hand in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  The yen strengthened in Tokyo as new data showed overall business activity in Japan holding steady in July, but a declining trade surplus underscored concern that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength could cap the embryonic economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Chief government spokesman Hiromu Nonaka said that the Finance Ministry and the BOJ need to keep in close contact with each other. \par \par \uc2  He stopped short of criticising the BOJ's decision on Tuesday to maintain current monetary policy amid intense pressure to ease and help weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  But he said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "should pay attention to the effects of current market movements on the Japanese economy." \par \par \uc2  The yen has climbed more than 14 percent since early July on the back of strong optimism over economic recovery, but politicians and the Finance Ministry have repeatedly warned that a strong yen could strangle the recovery. \par \par \uc2  The data released Wednesday showed the economy was holding steady. \par \par \uc2  The overall business activity index, released on Wednesday by the Trade Ministry and considered a proxy for the gross domestic product, was unchanged in July from the previous month, with a jump in industrial output offsetting a weaker service sector performance. \par \par \uc2  But Japan's trade surplus for August declined 22.6 percent from the same period a year earlier, marking the fifth consecutive month of decline and highlighting concern that the strong yen is dulling the appetite for Japanese goods overseas. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry, which compiles the trade data, stopped short of declaring a downtrend in the trade surplus, but Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda repeated his usual warning against a higher yen. \par \par \uc2  "The current situation shows an excessive yen appreciation," Kuroda told reporters Wednesday. "That is not good for Japan and the world economy." \par \par \uc2  Politicians soon joined in, with Ichizo Ohara saying that the BOJ's decision on Tuesday did not match the government's policy, and that he conveyed this view to the prime minister. \par \par \uc2  Ohara is a senior member of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party and an informal adviser to prime minister Keizo Obuchi, and is also an advocate of continued government spending. \par \par \uc2  Japan's economy is getting much of its support from public spending by the government and by sales of goods overseas, while consumers restrict their spending amid worsening job conditions. \par \par \uc2  The government, whose stability got a boost with the re-election of Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi as head of the ruling party on Tuesday, is expected to compile a four trillion to five trillion yen ($38-48 billion) spending package this year to maintain a steady stream of public spending. \par \par \uc2  Obuchi's administration is expected to submit an extra budget for the spending measures after carrying out a cabinet reshuffle. \par \par \uc2  On the international front, finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven (G7) nations will meet in Washington in three days, where the yen's rise is expected to top Japan's agenda. \par \par \uc2  Hirohisa Fujii, secretary-general and policy-chief of the Liberal Party that is in the ruling coalition with the LDP, said he thinks Finance Minister Miyazawa will request joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The BOJ was under massive pressure to ease monetary policy ahead of the G7 meeting to help Japan convince the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  to participate in concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Japan's economy is getting much of its support from public spending by the government and by selling goods overseas, while consumers restrict their spending amid worsening job conditions. \par \par \uc2  The government, whose stability got a boost with the re-election of Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi as head of the ruling party on Tuesday, is expected to compile a four to five trillion yen ($38-48 billion) spending package this year to maintain a steady stream of public spending. \par \par \uc2  Obuchi's administration is expected to submit an extra budget for the spending measures after carrying out a cabinet reshuffle. \par \par \uc2  On the international front, finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven nations will meet in Washington in three days, where \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues - and especially the yen's rise - are expected to be high on the agenda. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been under intense pressure to ease monetary policy to spur the economy and weaken the yen ahead of that meeting. \par \par \uc2  But it refused to budge at a policy-setting meeting on Tuesday and stuck to its current stance of providing ample liquidity to keep short-term money market rates at virtually zero. \par \par \uc2  The decision sent the yen sharply higher against the dollar to the 103 yen level overnight from just below 107 yen in Tokyo before the announcement. \par \par \uc2  A record $25.18 billion U.S. trade deficit in July, released by the Commerce Department overnight, also pressured the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The trade gap between Japan and the U.S. basically reflects the difference in strength between the two economies," Sawano said. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's Nikkei 225 stock average tumbled more than three percent on Wednesday morning to 17,369.78 as concern mounted over the high yen's effect on corporate earnings abroad and a drop-off in exports. \par \par \uc2  ($=105 yen) \par \par \uc2  ((Tokyo newsroom (813) 3432-8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9m03ai7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
