\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-BOJ stands firm, joint  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  hopes dim. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 749 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 1999\par 12:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 21 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan struck a rare blow for its independence on Tuesday, winning applause from advocates of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  autonomy but dimming prospects for joint U.S.-Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  Analysts disputed whether a BOJ decision to keep monetary policy unchange despite intense pressure to take aggressive steps to cap the yen's surge was best for the economy. \par \par \uc2  But many nonetheless agreed that sacrificing the hard-won principle of independence to political whims would have set a risky and unwelcome precedent. \par \par \uc2  "From a purely economic perspective, I think they did the wrong thing. A much more aggressive monetary policy is necessary," said Richard Jerram, chief economist at ING Baring Securities. "But from an institutional perspective, they did the right thing." \par \par \uc2  "The question is: should you allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make incorrect policy decisions if it wants to? It's difficult, but the answer must be 'yes' if you accept that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a better policy-making institution than politicians." \par \par \uc2  In the short term the BOJ's decision appeared to have dampened hopes that Washington would soon join Tokyo in jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prop up the dollar and weaken the yen, a point even BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami acknowledged in a news conference after the Policy Board meeting. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the United States does not share Japan's alarm over the yen's recent rise. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the United States is at the point where it considers even 105 yen to the dollar as particularly extreme," said Michael Wilkins, manager of cross market dealing at Credit Lyonnais. "Japan sees it that way...but they still need to convince their trade partners that this is a level that justifies any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Comments by finance officials and politicians had led markets to believe a BOJ easing was a prerequisite for getting the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other Group of Seven partners to jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  Critics said the public campaign to raise such expectations had in fact backfired, as the yen rose sharply following the announcement the BOJ's nine-member board, at a meeting on Tuesday, had decided to leave monetary policy unchanged. The yen was later moving at around 104.74 to the dollar in morning London trade, up from about 106.93 before the BOJ announcement. \par \par \uc2  "Basically, Japan is not very good at managing market expectations," Lyons said. "They tend to lead the market to water and then not allow the market to drink." \par \par \uc2  The latest attack on the BOJ's independence - sparked by a surge in the yen which ruling politicians fear could kill Japan's nascent economic recovery - came only 18 months after implementation of a law to free the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Finance Ministry's sway. \par \par \uc2  Critics had portrayed the BOJ's decision in February to guide short-term money market rates to nearly zero as a craven bow to political pressure. Yet fans of "quantitative" easing, which targets money supply, saw it as a welcome first step. \par \par \uc2  This time again ruling politicians, loath to see the recovery scuttled, joined the Finance Ministry in putting pressure on a reluctant BOJ to ease. Such action, officials said, might persuade an equally reluctant Washington to jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the BOJ board explicitly rejected calls to leave surplus funds from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market - "unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " - and ruled out using monetary policy as a tool to target \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. \par \par \uc2  Some private economists said the BOJ was right to resist pressure, not just to make a point but to forestall even more damaging attacks on its independence in the future. \par \par \uc2  "Just at the point where it seems like politicians and policy-makers wish to have a tame piggy bank at their disposal, the BOJ...doesn't want to play," said Chris Calderwood, chief economist at Jardine Fleming Securities. \par \par \uc2  "You need someone to fight off the politicians or you will just get a bunch of quick fixes that could damage the economy in the long term." \par \par \uc2  Others, however, noted that the door was still open to more aggressive policy easing once the BOJ had defended its principles in public. "Even if they loosen in two weeks time, they've made their point," Jerram said. ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8617 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9l00cms\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
