\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CROSS-Tenuous euro rally relies on BOJ largesse. \b0\par\par\uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 1999\par 06:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 20 (Reuters) - The euro kept a tenuous grip on 110.00 yen on Tuesday after an early bounce but its fate rested squarely in the uncertain hands of the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is under intense pressure to abandon all restraint and adopt a quantitative easing in money supply, both to counter the yen's strength directly and to enlist the support of the U.S. for joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Speculation of this had seen the euro rally sharply from last week's lifetime low around 106.76, but the euphoria tailed off overnight as market began to doubt the BOJ's commitment to easing and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' appetite for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  That left the euro in two-minds at 110.77/98 yen at the midsession here, up from a 109.65 low in New York but down on Tokyo's Monday finish of 112.56. \par \par \uc2  Traders said volumes were very light with the market spending all its time trying to second guess the BOJ. All felt that only a significant easing would reverse the yen's uptrend, and doubted the BOJ was prepared to go that far. \par \par \uc2  Bold steps included a commitment to unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or to underwriting JGB issuance. \par \par \uc2  But analysts noted that in both cases it would effectively place control of the money supply in the hands of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and turn the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  into little more than a rubber stamp institution. \par \par \uc2  More likely steps were to fatten the already massive daily surplus in the money market and increase purchases of longer data issues, so taking pressure off the longer end of the yield curve. \par \par \uc2  This would have some symbolic effect, said analysts, but would not necessarily depress the yen as much of the extra funds provided were expected to remain in Japan. \par \par \uc2  There was considerable speculation the bank could cut the 0.5 percent discount rate to 0.25 percent. This would not mean much in practice as the rate is largely redundant these days, but it could capture a few headlines and help deflect political pressure from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  And there was also a risk the BOJ board would decide to do nothing at all. Only two weeks ago the nine member board voted 7-2 to keep policy unchanged and one even argued for a tightening, so in theory at least an easing is not a foregone conclusion. \par \par \uc2  As far as the market was concerned, dealers suspected the yen would only be contained by a major quantitative package which included unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In such an event the euro was expected to surge past Monday's 112.70 yen high and they saw scope to reach the 114.80/115.00 resistance area before the day was out. \par \par \uc2  Equally, a modest move by the BOJ would likely see support around 109.65 tested though the market will be cautious of going aggressively long in the unlikely event the weekend G7 meeting yielded some sort of deal on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  If the BOJ left policy completely unchanged it would put paid to all talk of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and send the yen speeding higher, with 108.80 yen a first target followed by last week's lows under 107.00. \par \par \uc2  The euro could derive some support from the latest German IFO survey of business confidence which is due later today. \par \par \uc2  Median forecasts are for a continued improvement in the main index to 94.4 from 93.8 in July. Traders said there were rumours the index had been leaked and would actually top 97.0 in August, but many were sceptical noting that this figure always attracted rumours and they were nearly always wrong. \par \par \uc2  The talk certainly failed to lift the euro on the dollar, where it was idling at $1.0376/86 compared to $1.0436 late here on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Swiss Franc slipped to 68.89/94 yen from 69.97/02, while sterling sank to 173.04/16 yen from 174.89/01. ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9l00b2h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
