\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Yen pulls back, BOJ meeting in focus. \b0\par\par\uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 1999\par 10:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 20 (Reuters) - The yen was on its heels in early European trading on Monday as speculation grew that Japanese authorities might be gaining support from their Group of Seven counterparts to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. \par \par \uc2  The dollar and the euro retained most of their overnight gains to one-week highs against the yen as the market awaited Tuesday's Bank of Japan's policy board meeting. Traders said any move toward a further credit easing would be seen as a first step toward concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Unless we see some comments to the contrary - Japanese officials downplaying the room for the BOJ to do anything on monetary policy - I think the market will basically trade in fairly narrow ranges and see what comes out this evening," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 107.66/71 yen at 0715 GMT after rising to a one-week high of 108.16 yen overnight from 106.97/02 yen late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was last at 112.31/41 yen after touching a one-week high of 112.76 yen, compared with 111.29/39 yen late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market's belief now was that the BOJ would soften its opposition to a further credit easing in order to persuade the world's major economies - in particular, the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  - to agree on concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the upcoming G7 meeting scheduled for Saturday. \par \par \uc2  Speculation was the BOJ could either adopt so-called sterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in which it refrains from draining excess money market liquidity which results from its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or what is known as "quantitative easing", whereby it injects liquidity into the market to achieve certain inflation targets. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi said in a television programme on Sunday that Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa and Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami were studying various steps including quantitative easing. Obuchi also said joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with other countries was necessary to effectively counter the yen rise. \par \par \uc2  Traders noted there was also talk Japan would unveil a larger-than-expected fiscal package in order to convince its G7 partners it was doing all it could to sustain its economic recovery - although some traders noted that such stimulus measures were on their own yen-supportive unless they acted as a catalyst for concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Even if the BOJ did not announce any policy changes on Tuesday, the market would be likely to remain on its toes until the G7 meeting, traders said. \par \par \uc2  For the moment, the bounce in the dollar and the euro has taken some pressure off persistent sellers like Japanese exporters and investors, noted a Japanese bank trader in London. \par \par \uc2  "The vicious cycle where they sell because the dollar's falling and the dollar falls because they're selling has been snapped," he said. \par \par \uc2  The euro was slightly firmer against the dollar, supported by Bundesbank council member Franz-Christoph Zeitler's comments at the weekend that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be well advised to raise interest rates to pre-empt inflation. \par \par \uc2  The Welt am Sonntag newspaper quoted Zeitler on Sunday as saying "if you take your foot off the gas earlier, you don't have to brake so hard." \par \par \uc2  While a majority of the market did not expect the ECB to raise interest rates at Thursday's governing council meeting, the prospect of higher rates down the road could help keep the euro firm, Barrow at Bear Stearns said. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at $1.0436/41, up from $1.0406/08 late in Europe on Friday. ((Shin Kishima, London Capital Markets +44-171-542-6721, shinichi.kishima@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9k00hnq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
