\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr up on speculation of US-Japan action,BOJ steps. \b0\par\par\uc2 819 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 1999\par 09:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 17 (Reuters) - The dollar was sharply higher against the yen in late afternoon trading on Friday in Tokyo, lifted by speculation of possible joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and possible credit easing steps by the Bank of Japan (BOJ). \par \par \uc2  Fuelling such speculation was a morning comment by Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa, who said he thought it was possible that his deputy Haruhiko Kuroda had discussed joint \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with his counterpart at the U.S. Treasury during a meeting Thursday in Washington. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 106.69/74 yen at 0613 GMT, up from 105.05/15 yen in New York late on Thursday. It briefly rose as high as 106.82 yen. \par \par \uc2  Earlier the dollar slipped as low as 104.75 yen after the meeting between Kuroda, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, and U.S. Treasury Undersecretary for International Affairs Timothy Geithner produced no hint of joint action on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar reversed direction after Miyazawa hinted that the two officials may have discussed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Asked if Japanese and U.S. financial officials had discussed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Miyazawa told a news conference: "No one is saying so, but I think it is possible that they are discussing the issue." \par \par \uc2  That comment triggered dollar buying by foreign speculators who had sold the dollar short in expectations it would continue to fall against the yen. \par \par \uc2  "As Miyazawa seems to be deliberately dropping hints of possible joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , overseas fund operators actively squared dollar shorts," said a dealer at a city bank. \par \par \uc2  The yen was also weighed down by speculation that the BOJ might consider further credit easing steps, including "unsterilised" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Sterilisation refers to draining from the money market all the yen spent by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying dollars. The money is withdrawn to prevent an increase in money supply. \par \par \uc2  If a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not sterilise \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that increases the amount of money in circulation, constituting a defacto easing of monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Speculation that the BOJ might be persuaded to conduct unsterlised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  mounted after Miyazawa and BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami met on Friday morning for the second time in less than 24 hours. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa declined to comment afterwards on issues related to BOJ monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The Nihon Keizai Shimbun, Japan's leading business daily, reported on Friday that the BOJ was expected to notify the Group of Seven industrial powers next week that it would take "quantitative easing" steps, in addition to its current zero interest rate policy. \par \par \uc2  The paper cited no sources for its information. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi said on Friday that he was aware of "influential opinions" favouring a further monetary easing by the BOJ, but added that monetary policy is the prerogative of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  With pressure building on the BOJ to take further action to weaken the yen, much attention is focused on a meeting next Tuesday of the BOJ's nine-member policy board. \par \par \uc2  "Ahead of the BOJ's Policy Board meeting, easing speculation of could help underpin the dollar against the yen," said a dealer at another city bank. \par \par \uc2  In addition to overseas fund operators, Japanese trust banks were detected actively buying dollars below 105 yen, apparently to unwind hedge dollar sales, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Most economists polled by Reuters in London on Thursday thought it unlikely that the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  would join Japan in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb the yen's advance. \par \par \uc2  Of 28 economists polled, 15 put the chances of U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the coming month at below 50 percent. Only eight rated the likelihood above 50 percent while five rate it at 50-50. \par \par \uc2  But a growing number of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders in Tokyo believe the possibility of joint U.S./Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is increasing. "Most people had been sceptical about joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a dealer at a European bank in Tokyo said. "But that sentiment seems to have change over the last few hours." \par \par \uc2  In fact, he added, the market's disappointment would be great if joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or BOJ easing failed to materialise. \par \par \uc2  But some dealers continued to doubt that Washington will yield to Japan's request for help, as the yen's appreciation was not a pressing issue for the United States. \par \par \uc2  Of more urgent concern to U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers were allegations that loans to Russia extended by the International Monetary Fund (IMF) may have been misappropriated, with the crime concealed through a headline grabbing money-laundering scheme, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Summers is scheduled to testify in congressional hearings, which start Tuesday, on Russian money laundering. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the euro was at $1.0364/68 at 0655 GMT, little changed from $1.0370/75 in late New York on Thursday. ((Kanta Watanabe, Tokyo Treasury Desk, +81-3-3432-9780 tokyo.news room@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9h00ec8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
