\par\par \b \uc2  POLL-US unlikely to join  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Stamp \par \uc2 724 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 1999\par 15:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 16 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to join Japan in market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen in the next few weeks, according to a Reuters poll of economists on Thursday, but that view is far from universal. \par \par \uc2  Of the 28 economists in the poll just over half - 15 - put the chances of U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the coming month at below 50 percent and eight above. The other five rated it at 50-50. \par \par \uc2  Tony Norfield, head of global treasury research at ABN Amro in London, said history suggested the U.S. would shun any Japanese request for concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In the past, the Federal Reserve has very rarely bought dollars for yen and has not done so since 1995. "They have a track record of doing nothing. That would make you sceptical," said Norfield, who put the chance of U.S. action at 25 percent. \par \par \uc2  Rob Hayward at Bank of America in London felt the same way. "I don't think that the U.S. is overly concerned at the moment with the strength of the yen and weakness of the dollar. It's not on the Treasury Secretary's list of priorities," he said. \par \par \uc2  The yen shot up from around 122 to the dollar in mid July to a 3-1/2 year high of 103.20 on Wednesday, although it slipped on Thursday as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears crept into the market. \par \par \uc2  Economists who feel U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely - the Bank of Japan's lone attempts have been unsuccessful so far - say the issue is primarily Tokyo's domestic problem. \par \par \uc2  On a trade-weighted basis the dollar remains reasonably robust. Peter Stoneham at Thomson Global Markets said Washington would also want to see more signs of Japanese willingness to stimulate its economy before acting on the exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  ECONOMISTS SEE ANY \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  AT 100 YEN \par \par \uc2  The "no action" view held sway in the poll: the median chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was 40 percent. Respondents believed any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would come at about 100 yen to the dollar, although the speed of any climb would be more significant than the level. \par \par \uc2  More economists expected any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a meeting of G7 finance ministers around September 25 rather than before it. \par \par \uc2  Japan, worried the yen's strength will kill off its economic recovery shortly after birth, is sending the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top official to Washington to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves with other countries. \par \par \uc2  A number of economists said that if the United States was not worried about the Japanese recovery fizzling out, then it should be. \par \par \uc2  "A strengthening Japanese economy is in everbody's interest, including the United States'," said Howard Archer at NatWest Group, who put the chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 50-50. \par \par \uc2  James McKay of National Australia Bank said Washington had indeed a domestic interest in curbing the yen: preserving stability on its own asset markets. \par \par \uc2  FUNDS HEAD FOR TOKYO \par \par \uc2  With Japanese markets picking up due to the economic recovery, and fears growing of a major correction, especially on the U.S. equity market, funds are heading to Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "We're seeing a lot of Japanese repatriation of capital; the world wants its money back from the U.S.," said McKay. Many fund managers in the United States and Europe were underweight on Japanese investments and therefore underperforming, so they were joining the rush to buy Japan. \par \par \uc2  Some economists said dampening the yen would make Japanese assets less attractive and help to avoid a major withdrawal of funds from U.S. assets. That in turn could help to prevent any tumble on U.S. markets, which would hit the wider economy. \par \par \uc2  Adolf Rosenstock said Washington still needed to recognise that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in its interests but it may yet do so. \par \par \uc2  "After the (G7) meeting I think the probability for such a joint action increases very much. If it were to be successful, there would have to be American participation and not a little but a lot," he said. "It's really something that (should) leave a large impression on the market." \par \par \uc2  - Additional reporting by Penny MacRae and Hament Bulsara \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FOR DETAILS OF THE POLL PLEASE DOUBLECLICK ON\tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Polling Unit +44 171 542 5223 fax +44 171 542 4939, david.stamp@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9g00l0z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
