\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Japan has few options on yen's surge. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tatsuo Ito \par \uc2 797 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 1999\par 10:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 15 (Reuters) - The Japanese government has few options to rein in the runaway yen, economists say, especially since it seems unlikely to get help from the two quarters that could help the most - the Bank of Japan and the U.S. Treasury. \par \par \uc2  The government has allowed the yen to strengthen steadily since late July on signs of improvement in the long-stagnant Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  But they are worried that everything could be derailed by a sharp rise in the yen, which would hit earnings of the export-driven big industrial companies. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been selling yen on behalf of the government, entering the market on Friday and Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But the BOJ refuses further, radical monetary easing and Washington has shown no inclination to join Tokyo in a yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Economists say that barring such aid, the Japanese government can only hope to smooth the pace of the yen's ascent, which has knocked the dollar to repeated three-year lows. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese monetary authorities are feeling helpless after Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  proved unsuccessful," said a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  The greenback, which logged a high for the year of 124.75 yen in May has slid on an outpouring of data showing Japan emerging from its worst postwar slump. \par \par \uc2  After a brief Friday rally, the dollar slipped as low as 105.30 yen on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  But BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami on Monday explicitly refused calls from some economists and politicians to leave its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  "unsterilised" by not draining the excess yen proceeds from the money market. \par \par \uc2  Advocates of such a move say it would weaken the yen's value by leaving an oversupply of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and amount to a further easing of monetary policy, in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already driven short-term rates essentially to zero by pumping funds into the money market. \par \par \uc2  But Richard C. Koo, senior economist at Nomura Research Institute, agreed with the BOJ, calling the sterilisation argument "totally meaningless" as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already injected so much funds into the market that participants sometimes can't absorb it all. \par \par \uc2  A Finance Ministry source said that, despite some talk to the contrary, the U.S. Treasury also does not think the BOJ needs to stop sterilising its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  For Tokyo, the worry is not just the yen's value against the dollar but the greenback's value globally. \par \par \uc2  Policymakers fear an across-the-board drop by the dollar could cause non-U.S. investors to pull out some of their massive investments in U.S. securities, battering Wall Street and causing a hard landing for the soaring American economy. \par \par \uc2  This could in turn have a devastating effect on Japan's economy as well as other nations' economies, as the International Monetary Fund pointed out in a recent report. \par \par \uc2  "The biggest risk for Japan's economic recovery for the next six months to one year is a hard landing of the U.S. economy," said Yasuyoshi Masuda, senior economist at Fuji Research Institute. \par \par \uc2  "The problem is a narrowing economic disparity between Japan and the United States that had once supported a strong dollar by letting market participants shrug off the large U.S. trade deficit," Masuda said. \par \par \uc2  But as long as such a sharp U.S. economic slowdown or plunge on Wall Street fail to emerge, the United States is seen as highly unlikely to take immediate action on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A weak dollar makes U.S. goods and services more competitive overseas and could help curb the record high U.S. trade surplus from falling, thus soothing U.S. protectionism. \par \par \uc2  President Bill Clinton showed little concern on Friday about downward pressure on the dollar as a result of Japan's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "I know some in America are worried. They're afraid that a resurgent Japan means more competition for money and more pressure on the dollar," Clinton said. \par \par \uc2  "But on the whole, a Japan that could buy more American products and buy more products...from other Asian countries would be very much good for the global economy and therefore good for America's working people." \par \par \uc2  Nomura's Koo said the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  currently neither opposes nor backs single-handed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  To get direct cooperation from the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Koo said, Japan would have to take further fiscal steps than those already promised to show its resolve to stimulate domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  "Probably, 10 trillion yen ($95.2 billion) in real spending is necessary to get help from the United States to stem the yen's rise," Koo added. ((Tokyo Newsroom, +813 3432-8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9f00l03\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
