\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar lower in US, hurt by data and yen's luster. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 589 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 1999\par 16:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 14 (Reuters) - The dollar tripped lower soon after New York trading got underway on Tuesday when strong U.S. economic data spelled trouble for Wall Street by sparking fresh fears the Federal Reserve may soon need to tighten rates more. \par \par \uc2  Also, news that America's current account reached a record deficit of $80.67 billion in the second quarter accelerated losses, sending the dollar to its lowest level in more than three years against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Ongoing optimism about Japan's economic recovery has helped the yen retain its luster even though the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  again sold yen for dollars overnight in efforts to curb recent volatility. \par \par \uc2  "There is no fighting the fact that the U.S. economy shows more signs of strength and that will be disconcerting to the asset markets and could have a further negative impact on the dollar," said Rick Barnett, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Royal Bank of Canada. \par \par \uc2  Sales at U.S. retail stores surged 1.2 percent in August to post the strongest gain since February, easily topping Wall Street's forecast for a 0.8 percent rise and July's upwardly revised 1.0 percent gain. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders interpreted the data to suggest this year's two U.S. interest rate hikes, aimed at preventing economic overheating, have not done enough to cool growth and that another hike may need to be announced in October. \par \par \uc2  The news was negative for interest rate sensitive stocks; the Dow Jones industrial average started the session off more than 60 points at 10967.32. \par \par \uc2  Although the dollar started U.S. trading slightly firmer against the euro, it soon slipped to 1.04 dollars per euro from its 1.0362/70 dollars per euro starting level. The pair ended Monday trading at 1.0421/29 dollars per euro. \par \par \uc2  The dollar lost ground to the yen, starting at 106.44/54 yen and then quickly dropping below the psychologically key 106 yen level to a fresh three year low at 105.50 after ending Monday's session at 106.64/72. \par \par \uc2  Overnight the Bank of Japan again sold yen for dollars but because the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was reported to have been smaller than last Friday's move and because Japan still seems unwilling to conduct unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by leaving the money in the system permanently, the effect was limited. \par \par \uc2  But ongoing fears of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the possibility that Europe or the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  may at some point decide to join forces with Japan could limit action on Tuesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "We are near some critical levels and people may want to hang back a little," a dealer said. If the dollar should break below the 105.65 yen area, strategists said the path would be clear for a test of new historic lows below 80 yen. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the dollar also sank against the Swiss franc, tumbling to 1.5408/18 in early trading after gaining overnight to start at 1.5471/81 from 1.5402/12 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The British pound slipped to $1.6014/24 at the start of trading but then curbed its losses by rising to $1.6040/50 in early trading. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose against the Canadian dollar, rising to C$1.4695/05 from C$1.4685/95. The Australian dollar slipped to $0.6544/51 from $0.6564/71. \par \par \uc2  ((North American Treasury Desk (212 859-1639)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \pa