\par\par \b \uc2  U.S. does not want dollar drop - Japan's Sakaiya. \b0\par\par\uc2 385 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 1999\par 07:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 12 (Reuters) - Japan's Economic Planning Agency Minister Taichi Sakaiya and former Finance Ministry official Eisuke Sakakibara agreed on Sunday that the United States does not want the dollar declining further against the yen. \par \par \uc2  But while Sakaiya was cautious about the effectiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Sakakibara said the Japanese government needed to block a rapid and excessive rise in the yen. \par \par \uc2  "A rapid rise in the yen is undesirable," Sakaiya said on a TV Asahi programme. \par \par \uc2  "But the yen's rise is partly due to recovering confidence in Japan's economy and we should consider the effectiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with caution," Sakaiya added. \par \par \uc2  Speaking on the same programme, Sakakibara, a former vice finance minister for international affairs and currently a Keio University professor, said the United States did not want the dollar to weaken further. \par \par \uc2  He added: "(U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence) Summers has been saying the U.S. policy of supporting a strong dollar remained unchanged." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said he had spoken to Summers after the former Japanese financial diplomat resigned his post, and added: "I think Summers is willing to cooperate (with Japan)." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to block the dollar's fall when the greenback dipped close to 100 yen in the past, adding that Washington did not want a decline in the dollar below 100 yen as worries about a possible fall in U.S. share prices persist. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said there were views among market participants that the Bank of Japan (BOJ) thought the yen's rise was acceptable after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had privately criticised the government's decision on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in July. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ has said it would not further ease its monetary policy and the market thinks the yen's rise was acceptable by the BOJ," Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ's cooperation is important. Monetary policy and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy need to match. Although the BOJ is an independent bank, it should think about the national interest. \par \par \uc2  "If the BOJ acts with the government to avoid the yen's excessive rise, I think the United States would cooperate too," he added. ((Tokyo newsroom +813 3432-8018 email: tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9c00t4w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
