\par\par \b \uc2  Yen ends U.S. day higher after strong GDP data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Sternoff \par \uc2 739 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 1999\par 23:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 9 (Reuters) - Fresh signs of economic recovery in Japan sent the yen soaring on Thursday, pushing the dollar to a three-year low and the euro to an all-time low against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which held onto most of its gains in late U.S. trading. \par \par \uc2  "The yen's just being bought across the board," said Ben Strauss, vice president of Bank Julius Baer. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged 3.2 percent against the dollar and 3.6 percent against Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Japan shocked the markets with data showing an unexpectedly strong 0.2-percent rise in gross domestic product during the April-to-June quarter. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the euro was hit harder than the dollar because weaker German second quarter GDP figures cast a shadow over the recent spate of rosier economic forecasts for continental Europe. \par \par \uc2  "With Germany coming in with unchanged quarter-to-quarter GDP data and the Japanese number coming in strong, there is a glaring difference in the speed of recoveries," Strauss said. \par \par \uc2  After brushing a new three-year low of 107.58 yen in U.S. dealings, the dollar bounced slightly higher to stand at 107.83/88 in late U.S. trade after opening the U.S. session at 108.82/92. It closed Wednesday trading at 111.05/15. \par \par \uc2  The red-hot yen could cool a bit in the very near term, but gains are likely to heat up again in the next two weeks, said Paul Podolsky, strategist at Bank of Boston \par \par \uc2  "There are these very juicy technical targets that I think are going to be tested on the downside at about 105.75 in dollar/yen and near 112 in euro/yen." \par \par \uc2  Dealers remained extremely edgy about the possibility the Bank of Japan could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to put the brakes on the yen's rise. But they pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher after a top Federal Reserve official said the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not affecting the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  "I don't believe the change in the dollar/yen exchange rates has particularly significant implications for the U.S. economy," said Federal Reserve Bank of New York President William McDonough. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said McDonough's comments added weight to speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to join Japan in any possible future \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Tokyo has expressed concern that a strong yen could choke off Japan's recovery by making the nation's exports more expensive. \par \par \uc2  In late U.S. trading, Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  managed to find a foothold above its new lifetime low of 113.35 yen hit earlier in the session after tumbling from an overnight high of 117.60 yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro had also slipped against the dollar and British sterling in what traders said were the continuing effects of Wednesday's surprise interest rate hike by the Bank of England. \par \par \uc2  In late U.S. dealing, the euro hung close to session lows at $1.0540/47 after beginning U.S. trading barely changed from late Wednesday dealing at $1.0593/00. \par \par \uc2  "Sterling continues to be bought on the back of yesterday's rate hike," Strauss said. "People are buying (pounds), selling the continental \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buying English-speaking dollars in the crosses." \par \par \uc2  The British \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  climbed to a fresh four-month peak of $1.6374 before edging back to $1.6352/62 in late U.S. trading. \par \par \uc2  Sterling started in New York on Thursday at $1.6285/95 against $1.6180/90 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Thursday, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates on hold and its president Wim Duisenberg signalled that no move should be expected soon. \par \par \uc2  Traders became briefly excited when Duisenberg acknowledged that a tightening bias was creeping into the ECB's considerations at a "snail's pace", but the comment failed to lift the euro significantly before it slumped near $1.0550. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the greenback slipped against the Canadian dollar to C$1.4800/10 from C$1.4840/50 in early trading against C$1.4875/85 on Wednesday. The dollar edged up against the Swiss franc, trading at 1.5178/88 against 1.5100/10 in early U.S. dealing and 1.5093/03 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar gained to $0.6530/35 from $0.6501/11 early Thursday and $0.6476/81 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9900y50\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
