\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-Japan may need to act alone on  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Ohara. \b0\par\par\uc2 459 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1999\par 11:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 3 (Reuters) - Ichizo Ohara, a senior Japanese ruling party legislator and unofficial adviser to the prime minister, said on Friday Japan should be ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market without necessarily waiting until it can coordinate action with the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Japan should demonstrate its presence in the market and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone if it needs to, instead of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  verbally all the time," Ohara said in an interview with Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  Ohara added, however, that the United States might join Japan in preventing a further weakening of the dollar against the yen if it became nervous over an outflow of funds from the United States to Japan. \par \par \uc2  He said U.S. asset prices were close to an upper limit. \par \par \uc2  "Both U.S. and Japanese authorities are very nervous," Ohara said. "So we cannot rule out concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Ohara, an outspoken advocate of more expansionary monetary policies by the Bank of Japan (BOJ), repeated his view that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should ease monetary conditions further through so-called "unsterilised" \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in which yen sold for dollars in an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  are left in the money market. \par \par \uc2  "Why is the BOJ absorbing the yen released into the market after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?" Ohara said. \par \par \uc2  "I don't understand. They should not stick to the policy of sterilisation." \par \par \uc2  Regarding the size of an additional supplementary budget expected for the fiscal year ending in March 2000, Ohara said, "It is safe to assume the extra budget will be between five and 10 trillion yen." \par \par \uc2  Japan's Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa has repeatedly said the government would decide the size of the extra budget after examining gross domestic product data for April-June, due on September 9. \par \par \uc2  Ohara also reiterated his view that the BOJ should directly underwrite Japanese government bonds (JGBs) to prevent a further rise in long-term interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ should not let long-term interest rates keep on rising... We should prepare a legal framework to enable the BOJ to underwrite JGBs," he said. \par \par \uc2  But he added that Japan's economy does not need direct BOJ bond underwriting as badly as it did earlier this year, as Japanese firms now hold more unrealised profits on their shareholdings due to gains in the stock market, so they can afford to buy more JGBs. \par \par \uc2  Ohara also endorsed the idea of the BOJ adopting an inflation target to ease monetary policy further, and using a broader measure of inflation to achieve this. \par \par \uc2  "They could target a weighted average index between the CPI (consumer price index) and the WPI (wholesale price index)," Ohara said. ((Tokyo newsroom (813) 3432-8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv93018bv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
