\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ not mulling unsterilised  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  moves-Miyazawa. \b0\par\par\uc2 301 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 1999\par 05:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 31 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Tuesday he thinks the Bank of Japan (BOJ) as a rule does not consider conducting so-called unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Asked about news reports that BOJ Deputy Governor Yutaka Yamaguchi rejected calls for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market during his trip to the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Miyazawa said: "I don't know exactly what he said, so I cannot make a judgement. \par \par \uc2  "There are some economists who say the BOJ should leave yen funds, which result from (dollar-buying) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in the market. But I think the BOJ does not consider that as a rule." \par \par \uc2  He added that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would drain unnecessary funds from the market based on their judgement at the time. \par \par \uc2  Asked about the Japanese government's debt servicing costs for fiscal 2000/01, which are expected to be above 20 trillion yen, Miyazawa said he had expected such a size as Japan had put aside its fiscal reform plans last year. \par \par \uc2  He added: "If the economy gets out of its worst stage, we may reconsider fiscal reforms in the future. But now is not the time." \par \par \uc2  He also said Japan needed a supplementary budget for this fiscal year to next March if gross domestic product (GDP) for the April-June quarter turned out to be mediocre, although he declined to specify what he would consider a mediocre result. \par \par \uc2  Government officials have all but promised an extra budget, but have repeatedly said decisions on the size and timing of any extra budget would have to wait until the release of April-June GDP data, expected around September 10. \par \par \uc2  ((Yoko Nishikawa, Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8v01ida\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
