\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar buffeted at US noon by declines in US assets. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elizabeth Lazarowitz \par \uc2 749 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 1999\par 20:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 31 (Reuters) - The dollar raced lower to retest seven-month lows against the Japanese yen at midday on Tuesday after the early release of key data on U.S. manufacturing sent U.S. stocks and bonds tumbling. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had fallen sharply against the yen and the euro in overnight trading, but had recouped some of its losses early in the U.S. session. \par \par \uc2  But a steep decline in the Dow erased the dollar's modest morning gains against the yen, and pushed the greenback to a new one-week low against Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It's in reaction to the stock market," said Tomoko Iwakawa, vice president at Union Bank of California. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan's absence from the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for more than a month, despite the dollar's decline of more than 10 percent from its July highs, has encouraged yen buying. \par \par \uc2  The Dow's morning gains rapidly dissolved into a loss of more than 100 points and the benchmark 30-year Treasury bond was down about half a point on the day, bringing its yield to 6.1 percent, unnerved after strong U.S. manufacturing sector data. \par \par \uc2  The National Association of Purchasing Management's gauge of U.S. manufacturing activity, which was released a day earlier than scheduled, underscored a strong rebound in the industrial sector, hurt in the past year by weak export demand. \par \par \uc2  NAPM said its manufacturing index was rose to 54.2 in August from 53.4 in July. A reading above 50 indicates growth. \par \par \uc2  The dollar managed to find a toehold at its overnight low of 119.05 against the yen, before paring its losses slightly. \par \par \uc2  At 1300 EDT/1700 GMT, stood at 109.30/40 yen, nearly even with 109.25/35 in early U.S. trading and more than a yen below late Monday's level of 110.65/75. \par \par \uc2  But the greenback hung a mere one yen above January's key 108.25 low, a break of which would send the dollar to its lowest levels in some three years. Dealers said knock out options lurking below 109 could quicken a downmove in the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood $1.0594/96 at 1300 EDT/1700 GMT after vaulting to a fresh one-week high above $1.06 to stand more than one cent above late Monday's $1.0478/83 level and slightly firmer from $1.0582/89 in early U.S. dealing. \par \par \uc2  Thin summer market conditions, particularly ahead of the Labour Day weekend in the United States, were exaggerating the dollar's moves, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  While the market remained alert for any signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to dampen the yen's ascent, traders said the markets are still rearing to test the banks' resolve. \par \par \uc2  "The market feels Japan is very concerned, but they're not stepping in, and they're not doing any verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet," said Union Bank's Iwakawa, adding the market is waiting for a joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  involving Japan and either the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  or Europe. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have expressed concern that the yen's premature strength could damaged Japan's fledgling recovery. \par \par \uc2  Deputy finance ministers of the Group of Seven industrialised nations discussed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, but declined to divulge details, during two days of talks ending on Tuesday on broad economic themes. \par \par \uc2  Tepid Japanese economic data overnight failed to dent optimism about Japan's economic recovery, further encouraging yen bulls to continue snapping up the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yen's strength had also battered the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which dropped to a new lifetime low just below 115 in overnight trading, but the euro rebounded in early U.S. dealing, reclaiming a full yen. \par \par \uc2  Other European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also hit one-week highs against the dollar, following the euro's lead. \par \par \uc2  The British pound rose to $1.6085/95 from $1.6060/70 in early U.S. trading and from late Monday's $1.5895/05 level. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar tumbled against the Swiss franc to 1.5102/12 from $1.5292/02 in late Monday dealing, although it remained barely changed from early U.S. levels. \par \par \uc2  But technical factors lifted the greenback against the Canadian dollar to C$1.4945/55 from C$1.4893/03 in early U.S. trading, bringing it above late Monday's C$1.4935/45 level. \par \par \uc2  ((North American Treasury Desk, 212-859-1607)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8v01g7l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
