\par\par \b \uc2  Miyazawa - Didn't discuss specifics with Summers. \b0\par\par\uc2 506 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 1999\par 03:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 5 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Thursday that he spoke in general terms, not about specifics, when he discussed \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday with U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers. \par \par \uc2  "It's not that we discussed in that conversation what or how much we should do," Miyazawa said of the 20-minute phone call. "It was a general discussion, where we agreed to watch conditions and stay in touch." \par \par \uc2  The dollar rebounded from 5-month lows after Miyazawa announced on Tuesday that he and Summers had discussed recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements and agreed to keep in touch, as the markets saw this as a hint that the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  would join Japan in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost the dollar and curb the yen. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar has since come under renewed selling pressure as both sides, particularly the Treasury, have refused to follow up their terse Tuesday statements with fresh hints of concerted action. \par \par \uc2  But Miyazawa said at Thursday's news conference that nothing has changed about their intention to stay in touch. \par \par \uc2  He said he had asked his deputy, Vice Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda, again on Thursday to keep a close eye on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa rejected a suggestion that Japan's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are ineffective because the Bank of Japan subsequently "sterilises" them by draining from the Tokyo money market the excess yen that banks bought from the BOJ in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Some have suggested that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stop sterilising \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  both to make \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  more effective in weakening the yen's value and also as an unorthodox means of further monetary easing. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ is putting plenty of money into the market, so I don't agree with that reasoning," Miyazawa said. "This is a country with practically no interest rates." \par \par \uc2  The BOJ since February had driven the key overnight interest rate virtually to zero by pumping funds into the market, and other short-term interest rates have slid to similar levels. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa again praised this "bold" policy, saying it has been successful. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa also rejected a suggestion that, despite rock-bottom nominal interest rates, Japan's real interest rates were high because of deflation. \par \par \uc2  He acknowledged that the deflator used in calculating gross domestic product was negative, consumer prices flat to negative year-on-year and wholesale prices were negative. \par \par \uc2  "But I don't think it's right to say that therefore, real interest rates are high," he said. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa also: \par \par \uc2  * Praised U.S. moves to pay down its national debt with some of its recent fiscal surpluses, saying this was favourable for Japan but that he wanted to watch what effects it would have. \par \par \uc2  * Reiterated that the government was ready to use 500 billion yen in already budgeted reserves for public works spending if conditions warranted, without waiting to compile a second supplementary budget for this fiscal year. ((William Mallard, Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 email: tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8503aw1\par }\page {\par\fs20