\par\par \b \uc2  Fears of wobbly US mkt hurt dlr as US trade starts. \b0\par\par\uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 1999\par 15:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 4 (Reuters) - Bruised by investors' worries about wobbly U.S. asset markets, the dollar touched multi-month lows against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight and started U.S. trading broadly weaker. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar looks a bit softer and clearly people have been spooked by higher wage costs and the sense that there is more inflation in the system," John McCarthy, senior vice president at ING Barings Capital said, noting such worries were likely to exert more pressure on U.S. stock and bond markets. \par \par \uc2  Overnight, the dollar dropped one cent against the euro and lost a centime against the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  Since last week's news that U.S. employment costs jumped 1.1 percent in the second quarter, dealers have worried the Federal Reserve may need to hike interest rates sooner rather than later, possibly as early as August 24. \par \par \uc2  This has undermined the bond market, sending yields on the benchmark 30-year bond to 6.17 percent at Tuesday's close. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile euro/dollar started at $1.0760/65 after ending at $1.0680/87 on Tuesday while the dollar dipped to 1.4862/72 Swiss francs from 1.4962/72 francs. The British pound rose to $1.6235/45 from $1.6200/10. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen slipped to 114.68/78 from 115.22/32. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's decline may have been two-pronged, dealers said noting that stronger European economic data also weighed on the greenback. \par \par \uc2  German wholesale sales rose a real 1.2 percent in June from a year earlier and the German July services sector activity index rose to its highest in almost a year. \par \par \uc2  French consumer confidence index also rose to minus 8 in July from minus 10 in June. \par \par \uc2  Overnight the euro rose to a high of $1.0796, its highest since May 11, as stops were triggered near $1.0765. The next target is $1.08 while $1.0730 is strong support, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also touched its lowest level since April 15 against the Swiss franc and sterling rose to $1.6290, its highest level in nearly three months. \par \par \uc2  Against that backdrop, dealers remained very curious to hear what the U.S. Treasury may have to say about the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers is to attend the Treasury's quarterly refunding news conference this morning. \par \par \uc2  "Let's see what Summers has to say," said John Schein, vice president at Generale U.S.A. Financial Markets. "Even lip service from him would send the dollar up," possibly as high as 116 yen, he added. \par \par \uc2  Dealers remained particularly nervous about dollar/yen trading as the chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to overshadow the market. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have asked Japan to step up its reform plans in order to secure U.S. assistance with \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and noted that more talk about a new budget could be a step toward joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market moves. \par \par \uc2  But many dealers think Japan is alone in its efforts to pull in the yen and keep it from choking off recovery. "It looks to me they are on their own," Schein said. Technicians said dollar/yen is set to break below 114 before the weekend. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, the market is already eyeing Friday's employment data for more information about the labour market and growth which could be interpreted as new clues about the direction of U.S. interest rates. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the dollar rose to Canadian $1.4960 from C$1.4918/28, retracing some of its recent losses. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar opened at $0.6579/89 up from $0.6543/48. \par \par \uc2  ((-Svea Herbst-Bayliss, NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1639 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv840395x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
