\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar keeps footing on yen, slips elsewhere. \b0\par\par\uc2 704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 1999\par 07:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 4 (Reuters) - The dollar kept a tentative grip on 115.00 yen support at a nervous midsession on Wednesday as the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept speculators from tugging too hard. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  had failed to convince the market that the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to join it in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but there was a lingering fear of unilaterlal action, futile though it might be. That was enough to keep the dollar in a 114.80/115.30 range for now, they said. \par \par \uc2  Given this, the market was looking for safer ways to vent its bearishness and finding good buying opportunities in sterling, the Swiss franc and, to a lesser extent, the euro. \par \par \uc2  By 0330 GMT the dollar was poised at 115.02/07, having spent the entire morning in a tight 30 tick range after finishing at 115.22/32 in New York on Tuesday. The euro was benefiting from the dollar's discomfort and firmed to $1.0708/18 from $1.0680/87 late Tuesday with dealers reporting Japanese buying on the push past $1.0700. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bounced from $1.0625 support two days in a row now and analysts see it set to re-test last week's 2 1/2-month highs around $1.0745. A break of that would be bullish for a rally to the next major bull target at $1.0800 and could even herald a reversal in the euro's long downtrend. \par \par \uc2  As for the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , MOF officials were relatively quiet on Wednesday, with the vice finance minister for international affairs, Haruhiko Kuroda, merley saying that Japan and other nations' monetary authorities are in contact on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  as needed. \par \par \uc2  This followed U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers' confirmation overnight that he had talked about \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa. But Summers' statement pointedly omitted any mention of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or of any wish to see the dollar higher, so reinforcing the market's suspicions that the U.S. was in no rush to help Japan. \par \par \uc2  Without such assistance there was scant chance of Japan stopping the yen on its own - official data suggests the MOF has bought about $30 billion since June but the dolalr is still more than five yen lower. \par \par \uc2  Global capital flows were tending to favour Japan and, more recently, Europe as their economies looked to be recovering while the U.S. economy and asset markets were suspected of having peaked. \par \par \uc2  This in turn was breeding concerns about the willingness of offshore investors to keep funding the huge U.S. current account deficit, now running at over $20 billion a month. \par \par \uc2  If investors become reluctant to keep buying U.S. assets, interest rates there would tend to rise both to attract funds and to prevent an inflationary slide in the dollar. But higher rates would make the equity market look even more overvalued and could trigger a flight of foreign money from stocks, so depressing the dollar further. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say nightmare scenarios like this mean the U.S. authorities will be keeping a wary eye on the dollar and might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  should it suddenly collapse and the market turn disorderly. But at the moment, the dollar is still relatively firm in trade-weighted terms and the market very orderly. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, sterling was building on its recent recovery to reach $1.6211/21 from $1.6149/59 late in Tokyo on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The absence of any risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on this cross rate and a string of surprisingly strong British economic indicators are attracting broad-based demand, said dealers. \par \par \uc2  There was stiff chart resistance around last week's high of $1.6250 but traders looked for a break toward $1.6340 and eventually a re-test of May's $1.6430 peak. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc was gaining for many of the same reasons, strengthening to 1.4920/30 francs per dollar from 1.5013/23 on Tuesday. The 1.4930 level was considered key resistance and a clean break could see a quick move to 1.4850, analysts said. ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv84037ko\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
