\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar down at US close as asset mkts take toll. \b0\par\par\uc2 634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 1999\par 23:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 3 (Reuters) - The dollar limped broadly lower in U.S. afternoon trading on Tuesday, bruised by fresh investor sales as the stock market seesawed between gains and losses and the bond market hugged session lows. \par \par \uc2  "Sterling and the euro led the dollar's movement lower; basically it was an investor-driven move," Mellon Bank chief dealer Kevin Lawrie said as thin volumes dried up further. \par \par \uc2  The British pound jumped to $1.6200/10 from $1.6150/60 while euro/dollar bounced to $1.0680/87 from $1.0639/44. The Swiss franc sailed higher too with dollar/Swiss ending at 1.4962/72 from 1.5022/32. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the dollar closed unchanged at 115.22/32 yen, having erased modest losses just in time for the close. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar's smallest move of the day still provided the session's biggest talking point - the chance of more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers agreed. \par \par \uc2  Overnight the dollar surged nearly a full yen as Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa stoked speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  may assist Japan in future \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  when he told parliament that he and U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers had agreed that rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements were unfavourable. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa however remained mum on any concrete plans and Summers only said he would keep in touch with his colleagues. \par \par \uc2  "The headlines scared us, but then people read the story and realised they weren't saying anything we didn't already know," Paribas chief dealer Dennis Heidt said. \par \par \uc2  Consequently there were some dollar sales but they were modest, dealers said, explaining the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not evaporated even as many said Washington may not assist. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still in the back of everyone's mind and it is very hard to gauge what the Japanese may do, they have become craftier in their tactics," Lawrie said. \par \par \uc2  If there isn't more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon however, technicians said the dollar could sell off and break below the 114 yen level before the weekend. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar may pause for a day or two and even test higher, but ultimately I think we will be back near 113.60 and lower," Bank of America strategist Ture Johnson said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile many dealers also expected the euro to post more gains as Europe's economic landscape brightens and investors appear less enchanted with U.S. assets. Asset markets have been under pressure since last week when higher employment costs sparked talk of a possible rate hike in August. \par \par \uc2  While the benchmark Dow Jones industrial average closed 31 points higher on Tuesday, it had rolled between losses and gains for most of the session leaving some dealers dizzy. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers even began worrying whether the dollar's long reign as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of choice was coming to an end. \par \par \uc2  "As long as the U.S. was the only game in town ... smart investors were handsomely rewarded for investing in U.S. assets," Larry Hatheway, head of global strategy at Warburg Dillon Read said. \par \par \uc2  But with Europe and Japan showing signs of recovery, the group lowered its 12-month dollar forecasts. \par \par \uc2  In Britain, the construction sector expanded for the sixth straight month in July even though the pace of growth slowed. Dealers said this aided sterling even as dealers were biding their time before the Bank of England's MPC meeting on Wednesday and Thursday. \par \par \uc2  In other trading the dollar lost ground against its northern neighbour, falling to Canadian$1.4918/28 from C$1.4982/92 as a planned takeover was said to have fuelled demand for the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar ended at $0.6543/48, up from $0.6526/36. \par \par \uc2  ((-Svea Herbst-Bayliss, NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1639 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv83037l8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
