\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Rubin upbeat on U.S. inflation, rates. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Knut Engelmann \par \uc2 698 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 1999\par 21:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, June 28 (Reuters) - Outgoing Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin suggested on Monday he will not object to an expected rise in U.S. interest rates this week even though the inflation outlook for the world's top economy was benign. \par \par \uc2  Speaking to a small group of reporters just days before he was expected to leave office, Rubin reiterated his belief that the U.S. economy - booming in its ninth year of expansion - was likely to experience a period of low inflation ahead. \par \par \uc2  "I do think that's the most likely path ahead," he said. \par \par \uc2  Top Federal Reserve policy makers are scheduled to meet on Tuesday and Wednesday amid widespread expectations in financial markets that they will nudge up borrowing costs to prevent the nation's red-hot economy from overheating. \par \par \uc2  Rubin declined to comment directly on the Fed, as was his style throughout his more than four-year tenure at Treasury. But he suggested that he would not be against a move by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce the risk of future inflation since there was a question of whether current U.S. growth was sustainable. \par \par \uc2  "You've also got the question of sustaining our growth rate. I'm not saying that the rate we've had is sustainable or not sustainable. All I can think about is what policies we should be promoting in order to have the highest sustainable growth rate possible," Rubin said. \par \par \uc2  The economy expanded by a 4.3 percent annual rate in the first three months of the year, after a blockbuster 6 percent pace in the final three months of last year. But for now at least, inflation is still running at a modest annual rate of 2.1 percent, according to government figures. \par \par \uc2  He added that a slowdown in growth, which could be caused by higher rates that determine the cost of credit in the U.S. economy and beyond, would not necessarily be a bad thing. \par \par \uc2  "Slowing down doesn't necessarily mean that the economy is slower than it would otherwise be...maybe it's quite to the contrary, maybe that's the way to sustain growth," he said. \par \par \uc2  Rubin added that a possible slowing in the United States made it all the more important that other economies aim to keep their own growth rates robust. The U.S. administration has long been worried about a recession in Japan, the world's number-two economy, and a distinct slowdown in much of Europe. \par \par \uc2  Rubin reiterated that Japan should do more to stimulate its economy, and suggested that Tokyo's recent attempts to cap a rise in its yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which could hurt Japan's attempts at recovery by making its exports more costly - would be futile unless backed up by sound economic policies. \par \par \uc2  "I don't want to comment on their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. But I do think that the key in Japan, as it has been all along, is domestic demand, and having domestic demand grow on a regular and strong basis," he said. \par \par \uc2  At several occasions over recent weeks, Japan has attempted to curb the yen's strength by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and selling large amounts of its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not take part in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Rubin conceded that Japan had made progress over the last year in sorting out its debt-ridden banking sector and in using fiscal policy to help strengthen its recessionary economy. \par \par \uc2  But he added: "The only thing that is going to have an effect on any lasting basis is sound economic policies that are directed toward promoting consistent, strong domestic demand." \par \par \uc2  Rubin plans to step down in early July, once his designated successor and current deputy Lawrence Summers has been confirmed by the U.S. Senate. Summers has promised to carry on exactly where Rubin leaves off. \par \par \uc2  Rubin urged the Senate to move quickly to confirm Summers but declined to speculate whether he would stay on after July 4 - the date he has set for his departure - if there was any delay. "I don't think I should face that issue," he said. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6s05f5c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
