\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar rises vs. yen after  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 , dips elsewhere. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 427 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 1999\par 22:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Jan 12 (Reuters) - The dollar jumped nearly four yen Tuesday as Japanese officials tried to curb their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent gains, but it fell against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as Latin America appeared perched on the verge of a new crisis. \par \par \uc2  After days of speculation about how Tokyo may choose to tackle the yen's recent strength, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bank of Japan, bought dollars for yen below 110 yen and helped propel the dollar more than four yen higher. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  caught the markets off guard and certainly pushed the dollar higher against the yen today," said John McCarthy, senior vice president at ING Barings Capital. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 112.43 yen late in New York, up from 108.63 late Monday, near its lowest in 28 months. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat, Eisuke Sakakibara, accompanying Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi on a trip through Europe, further shored up the dollar when he said an "excessively strong yen is (as) undesirable as is an excessively weak yen." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not participate in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin later said there was no change in the Clinton administration's so-called strong dollar policy. He stressed the dollar would not be used as trade tool even amid growing tensions with Japan over steel imports. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar's gains were limited, dealers said, noting its decline against the Swiss franc and the new euro due to growing concerns about financial market turmoil in Latin America. \par \par \uc2  "What we had today was the dollar trading differently against the yen and the euro. It moved off the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on one hand and the Latin America problems on the other," said Rick Gomes, treasurer at Republic National Bank. \par \par \uc2  In Brazil, where one of the largest states recently imposed a debt moratorium and the government is embroiled in a fight about tax hikes, the stock market tumbled 7.6 percent. In Mexico the peso fell substantially and stocks slumped amid fears over deepening losses in Brazilian shares. \par \par \uc2  Investors worried in turn that weaker Latin American growth will hurt U.S. exports to the region and dent the dollar more. \par \par \uc2  The euro closed at $1.1565, up from $1.1500 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The British pound rose to $1.6305 from $1.6295 while the dollar slipped to 1.3872 Swiss francs from 1.4005. The dollar gained against the Canadian dollar to C$1.5135 from C$1.5060. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1c06ogw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
