\par\par \b \uc2  Central banks may act if $ goes below Y110-dealers. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kanta Watanabe \par \uc2 686 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 1998\par 07:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 2 (Reuters) - U.S. and Japanese financial authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the dollar if it slips below 110 yen, as its free-fall could throw the world into deep economic chaos, dealers and analysts in Tokyo say. \par \par \uc2  "Pressures on the dollar are mounting as no fundamental solutions are in sight for debt problems in Latin America, Russia and China," said Shigeo Ichioka, a strategist in the market trading department at Asahi Bank. \par \par \uc2  "A real crisis could start if the key reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  faltered. So there is a good possibility that central banks will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar falls below 110 yen," he added. \par \par \uc2  The level near 110 yen was crucial as Japanese investors could stampede to hedge against further losses if the greenback stayed below that level too long, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Senior officials of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as International Bureau chief Haruhiko Kuroda, have recently been keeping quiet about dollar/yen exchange rates. \par \par \uc2  Financial sources say authorities have not been too concerned over the current dollar level around 115 yen, but they were wary about a possible repeat of the dollar's 20 yen nosedive seen last month. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell as low as 111.45 yen on October 8 from a high earlier that week of 136.10 yen, as hedge funds hit by severe losses in emerging markets and on Wall Street sold dollars to unwind their yen carry trades. \par \par \uc2  The yen carry trade is a strategy through which fund operators borrow low-cost yen funds and invest them in higher-yielding instruments through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest-rate swaps. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said last week's comments by Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara underscored Japan's concerns over waning confidence in the U.S. economy leading to a global credit crunch. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara on Friday told a symposium on Asian economies that the United States, the world's largest economy, will be most vulnerable from now on due to its low savings rate and high fiscal debt. \par \par \uc2  The announcement last Friday by the Group of Seven industrial nations that they would provide additional resources of $90 billion to the International Monetary Fund was unlikely to suffice to calm fears of across-the-board dollar weakness, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "It remained uncertain whether Brazil could actually implement fiscal austerity steps demanded by the IMF after a strong showing by the opponents of such steps in the recent local elections," said Ryutaro Kono, senior economist at Dai-Ichi Life Research Institute. \par \par \uc2  Asahi's Ichioka said there were lingering fears that a debt crisis in China might force Beijing to spend some of the massive dollar holdings in its external reserves. \par \par \uc2  China had outstanding foreign debt of $137.96 billion as of the end of June, the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted by state Xinhua news agency as saying last Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The level of foreign debt roughly matched the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which stood at $141.1 billion as of the end of September. \par \par \uc2  Concerns over China's ability to honour foreign debt obligations have mounted since the shutdown of Guangdong International Trust and Investment Corp (GITIC) in October. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar if it weakened to below 110 yen, as that could hit U.S. asset markets and exacerbate a credit crunch, said Dai-ichi's Kono. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Japan also has domestic reasons not to want the yen to strengthen further, as it could intensify deflationary pressure on the economy, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government was studying ways to boost the economy through a second supplementary budget and the idea of handing out shopping vouchers to every citizen so as to boost consumption. \par \par \uc2  But Dai-Ichi's Kono said: "The recent yen gains have already wiped out positive effects expected from the proposed economic steps before they were finalised." \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 115.72/82 yen as of 0603 GMT on Monday. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk 81-3-3432-9780 \par \par \uc2  tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926dub205kvd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
