\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar slips against yen as  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  fear rises. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Al Yoon \par \uc2 495 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 1998\par 23:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 18 (Reuters) - The dollar lost ground to the yen on Tuesday amid growing speculation that Japan would try to reverse its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall with another bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the U.S. unit firmed against the German mark on lingering worries over Russia's political and financial woes, and was also propped by a larger-than-expected decline in the U.S. trade deficit in June. \par \par \uc2  In late trading, the dollar was lower at 144.92 yen, compared with 145.89 at the close Monday. It was modestly higher at 1.8013 marks from 1.7950. \par \par \uc2  Traders said talk that Asian central banks had been selling U.S. Treasury securities, perhaps in a bid to raise cash for an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , greased the dollar's drop against the yen along with more rhetoric from Japanese officials who warned of such an event. \par \par \uc2  "The curtailment of dollar-yen's upward momentum to some extent can be attributed to an increase in verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Japanese authorities," said Bob Lynch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Paribas Corp., who added "over time if it's not backed-up it'll have less and less of an impact." \par \par \uc2  Kiichi Miyazawa, Japan's finance minister, said Tokyo is always ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the yen. Earlier, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara noted market perception the yen hit a bottom of 147 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Japan last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , along with the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on June 17 after the dollar had raced more than nine yen higher over just two weeks. The dollar has since passed the 144.00-20 yen level where \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling was first seen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was little moved after the Federal Open Market Committee left U.S. short-term interest rates unchanged, as expected. Traders also said Monday night's mea culpa from President Bill Clinton regarding an inappropriate liaison with a former White House intern sparked no dollar reaction. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the dollar should garner more support against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as uncertainty over Russia's political situation unfolds. Some expected fireworks when the Duma meets Friday to tackle controversial anti-crisis measures put forth by the government of President Boris Yeltsin. \par \par \uc2  However, ongoing talk that European central banks have quietly sold dollars for marks at higher levels to stabilise the pair before the launch of Europe's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  union next year should foster a narrow trading range, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar drew modest support after the government said the U.S. trade deficit narrowed to $14.15 billion in June from $15.54 billion in May, but an initial push higher was short-lived. \par \par \uc2  The dollar in late trading was higher at Canadian $1.5282 from C$1.5275 on Monday. It was also up at 1.5102 Swiss francs from 1.5065, and sterling rose to $1.6166 from $1.6150. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8i011te\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
