\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr dented at US open after Jpn warns on weak yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 1998\par 15:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 12 (Reuters) - The dollar opened New York trading over 1-1/2 yen lower on Wednesday after dealers took profits overnight when Japanese officials warned that Tokyo will take an aggressive stance on its weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit also slipped against the mark as dollar/mark was pushed around by movements on the crosses, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "We have heard some very strong warnings from Japan and after the market reached danger levels Tuesday everyone feared that something was coming, so people began to take profits," explained John Schein, vice president at Generale Bank. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen slumped to 145.57/62 from 147.22/32 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the mark, the dollar opened at 1.7731/34 after closing at 1.7810/20 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers explained that consolidation of gains and profit-taking was expected after the dollar raced to an eight-year high after it broke past 146.75 yen on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  While dealers appeared modestly confident the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not assist in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately, they admitted to concerns the Bank of Japan could strike. \par \par \uc2  "There was talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and while we haven't seen actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we have seen the warnings about it," said Jeffrey Yu, senior dealer at Sanwa Bank. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara, known for his ability to move \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, warned Japan will take an aggressive stance on the yen. Dealers took particular note of Sakakibara's comments as he has been conspicuously silent in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  Also Haruhiko Kuroda, director-general of the Finance Ministry's International Bureau said the current rise of the dollar was excessive. \par \par \uc2  But traders paid the most attention to a news agency's report that an unnamed \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  Official had said a dollar/yen rate between 130 and 139 would be acceptable. \par \par \uc2  "The after effect of that is going to keep people on pins and needles for some time," Schein predicted. \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless U.S. dealers said the market appeared ready to sell yen again, albeit cautiously. \par \par \uc2  "The trend has not changed," Yu said referring to the market's scepticism about Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi's ability to tackle banking reforms and fiscal stimulus to rekindle economic growth. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said as long as 144.90/145.00 support holds, the dollar could probe the 146 area before encountering more resistance near the 146.75 level. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also said speculation about possible coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could heat up as a meeting between Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa and U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin scheduled for early September approaches. \par \par \uc2  As the market remains focused on the yen, dealers said dollar/mark movements occurred largely because of cross trading. "It is all based on the crosses," Yu said, after sterling had briefly slid toward the 2.8800-marks technical support before crawling back to the 2.8925 area. Sterling was hurt as Bank of England deputy governor Mervyn King said he expected sterling to fall particularly against European units. \par \par \uc2  Also the German DAX stock market index was up nearly three percent to top 5,400 points in after-hours trading, helping support the mark, dealers said. The market was also paying close attention to the Dow Jones industrial average, hoping for small gains. The Dow was up 70 points in early trade. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere the British pound slipped to $1.6282/92 from $1.6319/24 while the dollar eased to 1.4828/38 Swiss francs from 1.4908/18. The dollar also fell against the Canadian dollar to C$1.5120/25 from C$1.5220/25, in line with the dollar's sharp decline against the yen. The Australian dollar opened at $0.5980/90, up from $0.5934/39. \par \par \uc2  ((-Svea Herbst-Bayliss, NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1639 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8c02zx5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
