\par\par \b \uc2  China media urges Japan and U.S. to bolster yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 555 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 1998\par 06:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, Aug 12 (Reuters) - A Chinese newspaper on Wednesday urged Japan and the United States to take steps to stop the yen from sliding further, warning that continued yen weakness will trigger a new round of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations across Asia. \par \par \uc2  "The United States and Japan should adopt resolute actions to stop the downward trend of the Japanese yen to help some Asian countries navigate out of troubled water," the official Economic Information Daily said in a report. \par \par \uc2  It quoted economists as saying a new round of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations would hit Asia if the yen kept falling. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at 146.05/15 yen on Wednesday after rising to eight-year highs above 147 earlier in the week. \par \par \uc2  The newspaper quoted experts as forecasting most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would depreciate 5.0-10 percent if the yen hovered at 146-147 yen against the greenback for an extended period. \par \par \uc2  It slammed the new Japanese government for dragging its feet on bucking the yen trend. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese government's economic reform policies lack details, and its actions are not decisive, which is one of the factors causing the yen's prevailing weakness," it said. \par \par \uc2  "As one of the world's superpower economies, Japan ought to launch market-rescuing actions now for its strong economy." \par \par \uc2  "But according to its recent performance, it's obvious that Japan is unwilling to undertake the obligation," it said. \par \par \uc2  The absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  also fueled the yen slide, the newspaper said. \par \par \uc2  A signed article in the official Economic Daily on Tuesday said the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was on its way to the 150 level and urged Japan's government to "do something about the yen". \par \par \uc2  "Japan can easily stabilise the yen by adjusting its current excessively low interest rates, taking measures to limit the flow of yen out of Japan or directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markers," the essay said. \par \par \uc2  The Economic Information Daily said a weak yen and a strong dollar are in the interest of the two nations. \par \par \uc2  "The pros for Japan from a devalued yen far outweigh the cons as it can greatly enhance the competitiveness of Japanese products, so the Japanese government is reluctant to rein in the yen slide," the newspaper said. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. also does not like to see a swift rebound in the yen as a bearish yen is extremely beneficial for the U.S. to absorb overseas investment," it said, apparently ignoring repeated U.S. expressions of concern about Japan's economy and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The cheap Japanese products can also help alleviate inflation pressures in the United States," it added. \par \par \uc2  China, whose exports are staggering under increasing pressure from cheaper regional products, has been urging Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the international money market since the yen fell to around the 145 level. \par \par \uc2  Some Chinese economists and many exporters have pushed for a yuan devaluation to boost exports, but Beijing has showed no sign of easing its "no devaluation" stance. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy governor Liu Mingkang reiterated the policy at a press conference in Beijing on Tuesday, saying a drastic devaluation could bring about a short-term export recovery but it would not help China's long-term strategic competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8c02xeg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
