\par\par \b \uc2  The calm after the storm? \b0\par\par\uc2  By Richard Melville \par \uc2 685 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 1998\par 22:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 7 (Reuters) - The tumult in Wall Street stocks seems to have waned, meaning investors could enjoy a long overdue period of peace next week. \par \par \uc2  Given the market's recent volatility and the potential for an eruption on one of several economic and political fronts, investors are likely to view an onset of the summer doldrums - that lethargic period where volume dips to holiday-like levels - less as a calm after the storm than the eye of a hurricane. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average has staged a partial recovery from its nearly 1,000-point correction, a slide that peaked in intensity during Tuesday's 299-point slide. \par \par \uc2  Some on Wall Street were particularly encouraged by renewed late-week buying in downtrodded areas. \par \par \uc2  "We actually saw investors become a little more venturesome, with buying in areas like techs and energy," said Hugh Johnson, chief investment officer at First Albany. "It looks like there's a little more willingness to take risk and that's a very positive sign." \par \par \uc2  Also helping was a decidedly benign July wage and payrolls report on Friday, one that confirmed the economy remains on track for moderate growth, despite the ongoing Asian economic crisis. Importantly, that growth still has not sparked any hint of meaningful wage or price inflation. \par \par \uc2  "The shock from Asia has slowed the economy, but its only intrinsic problem is a potential shortage of workers," said Bruce Steinberg, chief economist at Merrill Lynch. "In that sense, a soft landing is just what the doctor ordered." \par \par \uc2  While questions persist about U.S. corporate profit growth in the near term, the overall economic climate has Steinberg optimistic about next year's earnings outlook. \par \par \uc2  While optimism gained the upper hand at the end of the week, most analysts stopped far short of forecasting a quick repair to the market's damage and to be sure, some see more lows before new highs. \par \par \uc2  For one thing, politics in Washington remain a large question mark, as former White House intern Monica Lewinsky may be called to add to her testimony before a grand jury about her relationship with President Bill Clinton. \par \par \uc2  Clinton is scheduled to offer his side of the story on Aug. 17 and rumours will undoubtedly swirl through the markets ahead of the event. \par \par \uc2  But analysts are less worried about the Clinton affair than the potential for more turmoil stemming from Asia. \par \par \uc2  "This makes for wonderful train conversation in the morning, but from an economic perspective it doesn't amount to a hill of beans," said Philip Orlando, chief investment officer at Value Line Asset Management. \par \par \uc2  "Everybody knows it's really (Treasury Secretary Robert) Rubin and (Federal Reserve Chairman Alan) Greenspan with their hands on the rudder of this economy," Orlando said. \par \par \uc2  In contrast, the weakness of the Japanese yen and the possibility of new regional problems remain the top concerns on Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  The yen's weakness has weighed on U.S. export levels, slowed Gross Domestic Product growth and already prodded central banks in the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japan to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of eyes will be on Asia and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  situation over there," said Charles Crane, chief market strategist at Key Asset Management. "If we get a devaluation out of Hong Kong or China, that takes all the focus off the U.S." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness has become an issue in countries far removed from Asia. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Canada - the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' largest trading partner - \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, aiming to end a slide in that country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Investors will receive a brief respite on the economic front, with no major data due until Thursday's retail sales report. Producer price data is slated for release on Friday. \par \par \uc2  For the week, the Dow was off 285.27 points at 8,598.02. The Nasdaq composite index was off 25.62 at 1,846.77. The Standard & Poor's 500 index lost 31.22 at 1,089.45. \par \par \uc2  ((-Wall Street desk, 212-859-1730)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8702q9t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
