\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar up at U.S. noon, Tsy urges Japan to reform. \b0\par\par\uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 1998\par 19:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, July 30 (Reuters) - The dollar stood higher at noon in New York as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers took cues from a rising U.S. stock market and ongoing concerns about Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2  "The stock market is doing better and that is helping the dollar out in relatively thin, summer volumes," said Andrew Chaveriat, analyst at Paribas. \par \par \uc2  Although the dollar opened lower against the yen, it quickly erased those losses and climbed further as the United States issued a stern warning to Japan to hurry reforms. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen at noon rose to 142.50/55 after opening at 142.00/10, and dollar/mark was up at 1.7748/58 from 1.7661/64. \par \par \uc2  The dollar moved in a relatively narrow 84 pip range during the morning and was rebuffed near 143 yen where traders became mildly worried about prospects for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  As they have for days, traders talked mainly about Japan's new government and whether it will push through true reforms by cutting taxes as promised, and tackling mountains of bad bank loans. Estimates from outside experts put Japan's bad debts near $1 trillion, almost twice the official estimate. \par \par \uc2  "That certainly did not help the yen," a trader quipped. \par \par \uc2  The United States also kept up pressure on Tokyo as Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers urged rapid reforms. \par \par \uc2  "We hope that the Japanese government will move rapidly and effectively in each of these areas (repairing the banking system, stimulate the economy with respect to deregulation) we have been stressing in our dialogue with Japan for some time," Summers said. \par \par \uc2  While dealers said the United States is clearly trying to hurry Japan's efforts, they also speculated Washington might still be willing to help Japan with potential \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially amid fresh talk that China may mull a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  "China seems to be pushing the envelope a bit," said one dealer who characterised the talk as sabre-rattling. \par \par \uc2  Analysts have said the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  agreed to the June 17 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in part, because China had threatened to devalue the yuan if the yen continued to slide against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Strategists at BankBoston however argued China's "case that a yuan devaluation is necessary if the yen continues to weaken seems flimsy" because trade statistics do not suggest Tokyo enjoys any sort of advantage from the weaker yen. \par \par \uc2  The yen's weakness was also visible on the crosses where the mark raced to 80.50 yen, looking set to probe 80.90 soon. \par \par \uc2  While the dollar did gain against the mark, dealers were not unanimously convinced more gains were ahead. \par \par \uc2  The dollar appeared to shrug off news that Fitch IBCA downgraded Russia's foreign long-term debt rating to BB-from BB, citing looming political confrontation over the 1999 budget and tax collection troubles. \par \par \uc2  "For the spot guys, Russia blipped off the radar as soon as they got the aid package. It could become a problem again, but it doesn't seem to be one now," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Instead, the dollar remained under some pressure earlier as foreign investors were concerned about the ongoing White House sex and perjury allegations. But as these were seen affecting the stock market more directly the impact was shrugged off. \par \par \uc2  "We are seeing a bit of a short-squeeze on dollar/mark," Chaveriat said, as the pair pushed former support at 1.7760. Now the next target appears to be 1.7860, a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the British pound nudged higher against the mark as well, which was helping underpin the dollar. It slipped to $1.6410/20 from $1.6430/40 however. The dollar also rose to 1.4855/65 Swiss francs from 1.4780/90. The dollar also firmed modestly against the Canadian dollar to C$1.5082/90 from C$1.5050/60. \par \par \uc2  ((-Svea Herbst-Bayliss, NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1638 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du7u02wx8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
