\par\par \b \uc2  Quiet dlr higher at US noon, Russia woes hurt mark. \b0\par\par\uc2 582 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 1998\par 18:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 29 (Reuters) - The dollar was higher in rangebound trading at midday, as turbulent Russian markets pressured the mark and Japan's poor fundamentals overcame near-term fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also stressed the U.S. unit maintained its overall bid tone as a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid continued turmoil in emerging markets around the world. \par \par \uc2  Russian markets led those concerns as the benchmark RTS index ended down 6.11 percent. A Russian rate rise on Friday failed to shore up investor sentiment that focused on Russia's urgent need for at least $10-$15 billion in additional aid. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the Russian government showed desperation over its cash needs on Monday as the nation asked the World Bank to accelerate the release of a $600 million loan not slated for disbursement until October. \par \par \uc2  Russia and the International Monetary Fund have disagreed over parts of a government programme to solve its fiscal crisis but a senior Russian negotiator said he was optimistic over the prospects for additional IMF aid. \par \par \uc2  "There's still so much uncertainty about Russia and that's why you see flight to the dollar," said Christer Stefansson of Swedbank, who added that dollar/mark was also technically bid. \par \par \uc2  At noon, the dollar was higher at 1.8140/50 marks, up from 1.8080/90 at the open but still shy of its overnight high at 1.8165. Dollar/yen was up at 141.85/95 from 141.73/83. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the mark was offered on the crosses, led by a sterling/mark bid back above 3.02, near the overnight and two-month high at 3.0243. Despite the mark selling theme dealers reported thin flows and doubted any major spikes. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/mark started going more bullish on Friday but it's always overshadowed by the moves in dollar/yen so it's hard to stay focused on it - overall its so quiet," said a trader at a French bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  Despite unrelenting desires to sell the yen, traders said aggressive yen sales were curbed by the ongoing chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Japan and Federal Reserve. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto awakened the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  card, saying that he is seeking cooperation from the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  and China to stabilise the yen. Hashimoto also said extreme yen fluctuations were undesirable. \par \par \uc2  Traders also speculated that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be paired with Japan's expected outline of its bridge bank plan on Thursday, which may be seen as the first step by Japan to confront its mountain of bad debts. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people got hurt on the last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and for that reason people haven't enough reason to play in dollar/yen now with Japan cooking up another plan," a trader said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen fell sharply overnight after the Bank of Japan's quarterly tankan survey of business sentiment came in at -38 versus forecasts for -42, or worse. The better-than-expected results still painted a dire picture of the Japanese economy, however, leaving forecasts for a weaker yen untouched. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the dollar was higher at 1.5263/73 Swiss francs from 1.5220/30 at the open and sterling was off at $1.6640/50 from $1.6665/75. The dollar rose to Canadian $1.4713/18 from C$1.4698/03. \par \par \uc2  ((-Al Yoon, N.A. Treasury Desk, 212-859-1672)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \