\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-S.African rand hits lows against dlr, pound. \b0\par\par\uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 1998\par 11:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 29 (Reuters) - The South African rand touched all-time lows against the dollar and pound on Monday morning as speculative pressures resumed following sharp losses on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The rand hit 6.00 against the dollar and 9.9714 against the pound, deepening losses on the dollar since Thursday morning to nearly nine percent. \par \par \uc2  Against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar was also strengthening on the yen in European morning trade as the impact of a better-than-expected Japanese business sentiment survey over night began to wear off and and drag the yen lower. \par \par \uc2  In European equity markets, most stocks got off to a good start, with Frankfurt touching a new trading high. \par \par \uc2  But most attention in early trade was fixed on South Africa's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has shed 26 percent since the end of last year as a deepening emerging market crisis has shaken investor confidence and made it an easy target for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Johannesburg reported unprecedented \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Britain and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday afternoon, but this failed to halt the headlong slide. Analysts fear that dwindling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves mean the Reserve Bank of South Africa is low on ammunition to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  South Africa's All Share index opened 0.7 percent lower on Monday on the back of the slumping rand and a weaker futures market. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was more robust against the yen. Even though there was some relief that Japan's key major manufacturers' index in the Bank of Japan's Tankan survey showed a less gloomy than forecast reading. However, at minus 38, it was still weaker than the minus 31 seen in the previous survey. \par \par \uc2  "The Tankan was very weak, especially for the smaller manufacturers which are exposed to the ongoing credit crunch," said Jesper Dannesboe, treasury economist at ABN-Amro in London. \par \par \uc2  Demand for the dollar was also strong in Karachi, where the Pakistani rupee slipped 0.40 to 51.40 to the dollar in the kerb at Monday's open after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  devalued the local unit by 4.2 percent, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjusted the rupee down to 46.00/46.46 to the dollar on Saturday from 44.0500/44.4905 fixed on March 24. "The dollar demand is high because people fear more devaluation of the rupee in the coming weeks," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Major European equity markets showed gains in early trade. Germany's Xetra DAX index rose above 5,900 points, setting its sights on the coveted 6,000 level but dealers warned against too much enthusiasm too early in the week. \par \par \uc2  By 0754 GMT the Xetra DAX was 0.65 percent stronger at 5,908.50 points, after hitting a record high of 5,926.88. "The goal is 6,000 because it looks so obvious that we're going there and it's only another two percent or so. But to be honest I'm not a big fan of this," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "We're more limited on the upside potential than the downside. But who knows," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  In London, shares moved higher shortly after the start of business on Monday, led by a rise in the futures contract but with little volume to back up the move, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The FTSE was up 22.9 points or 0.4 percent at 5,903.3, after initially jumping almost 50 points. The September future was up 23.0 points at 5,973.0. \par \par \uc2  Yet with little in the performance of international markets to give the market a clear lead, dealers said the index appeared unready to move out of its recent trading range broadly between 5,700 and 6,000. \par \par \uc2  French shares open ahead, the market supported by a strong German open and renewed positive signals about continental European shares, and with no immediate downside emerging from Asia, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6t01upl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
