\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar ends week higher in U.S. as crises linger. \b0\par\par\uc2 631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 1998\par 22:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 26 (Reuters) - The dollar ended a busy week higher against the mark and the yen on Friday as concerns about Russia's and Japan's financial crises prompted investors to seek safety in U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark ended at 1.8095/05, a full pfennig above its 1.7994/97 opening level while dollar/yen stood at 141.85/95, modestly above the 141.73/83 open. \par \par \uc2  Dealers however noted that closing prices failed to accurately reflect the day's events where one crisis after the next seemed to spawn an endless succession of rumours, leading to very nervous trading conditions. \par \par \uc2  "It was a totally crazy day and I am dead," lamented John Schein, vice president at Generale Bank. \par \par \uc2  News that Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates by 20 percentage points to 80 percent, effective Monday, cast a shadow over the mark, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Moscow's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took the step only one day after the International Monetary Fund agreed to unblock a stalled $670 million in aid to Russia. Still, the rouble still fell outside the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bands and domestic debt prices plummeted as dealers reported new fears that reforms may be stalled. \par \par \uc2  "The Russian situation has weighed on the mark," said Marc Chandler, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank Securities. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said it was not the only reason investors were exiting the mark, noting that the dollar was simply being seen as the safest investment at the moment. \par \par \uc2  "I am still very bullish for dollar/mark and a break of 1.8160 could signal another test of the year's highs around 1.8562," Chandler added. \par \par \uc2  Even as dollar/mark posted the day's larger gains, dealers had not lost sight of dollar/yen, still expressing intense interest in buying dollars for yen. \par \par \uc2  "I still think the bet is to buy more dollar/yen," another dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Investors expressed new disappointment with Japan's lack of reform efforts even as talk of a possible bank merger seemed to suggest new steps were being taken to overhaul the nation's frail banking system, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan Ltd. and Sumitomo Trust & Banking Co., Ltd. are mulling a possible merger. \par \par \uc2  "Japan remains the same, there is no trust policymakers are cleaning house. There is a lot of talk of reform but last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was only a Band-Aid," Schein said. \par \par \uc2  At the same time however, investors were frightened off from more aggressive dollar buying after Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, travelling in China with U.S. President Bill Clinton, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to closely monitor the timing for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week joined Japan and spent billions of dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stabilise the yen, buying yen for the first time in six years. Although the yen climbed six percent last week, gains have nearly evaporated this week. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve Bank of New York was also seen selling dollars for South African rand on Friday, acting as an agent. \par \par \uc2  Neither the Fed nor the Reserve Bank commented on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  which appeared fruitless as the rand stabilised only briefly before plummeting to a new record low at 5.9605 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  In other trading the British pound slipped to $1.6610/20 from $1.6655/65 and the dollar climbed against the Swiss franc to 1.5265/75 from 1.5159/64. The dollar also edged higher against the Canadian dollar to C$1.4693/98 from C$1.4650/55. The Australian dollar ended at $0.6018/25 down from its $0.6062/67 open. \par \par \uc2  ((-Svea Herbst-Bayliss, NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1639 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6q01yir\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
