\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar ends mixed as Japan crisis continues to weigh. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 422 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 1998\par 22:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 25 (Reuters) - The dollar climbed against the Japanese yen on Thursday amid concerns Tokyo may not be ready to tackle economic reforms but slipped against the mark on hopes that foreign aid will help stem Russia's financial woes. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed nearly a full yen higher, managing to cross the psychologically key 142 yen level for the first time since the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japan joined forces and spent billions on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilise the yen last week. \par \par \uc2  "The reasons for selling yen are the same today as they were yesterday," Marc Chandler, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell said, explaining the market's loss of patience with Japan's promises to tackle reforms aimed at stimulating growth. \par \par \uc2  After senior U.S. officials had urged Tokyo to push through new tax cuts, dealers were disappointed when Japan's Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto said recent tax cuts will help the economy, suggesting these cuts may not need to become permanent. \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto also appeared reluctant to force banks to disclose all loans that require monitoring and this sparked talk that Tokyo may not be ready to abandon its "convoy" system where strong institutions or the government rescues weaker firms. \par \par \uc2  Activity however was extremely nervous, particularly after talk that the Federal Reserve Bank or a large Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been selling dollars spread across trading floors in the early afternoon. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 142.07, up from Wednesday's 141.30 close. Against the mark, the dollar slipped to 1.7985 from 1.8025 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The German mark gained ground amid signs the International Monetary Fund would release a stalled $670 million loan to Russia in order to help keep the economy afloat. The fund announced the payout as New York trading was winding down. \par \par \uc2  Russia's financial crisis, the most severe in recent years, has harmed the mark in particular because of Germany's close relations with Moscow. \par \par \uc2  "The mark has nudged higher because people are optimistic the IMF won't let anything bad happen to Russia," Chandler said. \par \par \uc2  There was little market reaction to news Germany's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bundesbank, left interest rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the British pound rose to $1.6713 from $1.6683 as the dollar climbed against the Swiss franc to 1.5160 from 1.5080. The dollar gave up some ground against the Canadian dollar, slipping to C$1.4663 from 1.4698. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6p025tf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
