\par\par \b \uc2  Japan reforms key to stable yen-China newspaper. \b0\par\par\uc2 523 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 1998\par 10:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, June 19 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week to stop the yen's slide was beneficial to Asia, but could have only short-term effect without Japanese economic restructuring, warned a commentary in the official Financial News on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has taken effect, but factors behind the weakening yen against the U.S. dollar have not been fundamentally changed," the commentary said. \par \par \uc2  "The key to whether the yen will remain stable against the dollar lies with Japan itself," it said, echoing the sentiment of many analysts and markets in Asia. \par \par \uc2  "Only after structural reforms and a reinvigoration of the Japanese economy will the yen stabilise." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong on Thursday reaffirmed China's stance not to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and welcomed the previous night's U.S.-Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the slide of the battered yen. \par \par \uc2  Dai and other Chinese officials issued strong warnings that the yen's slide was putting immense downward pressure on the yuan. \par \par \uc2  Another Chinese financial daily on Friday quoted a senior bank official as saying China should draw lessons from the Asian financial crisis and take a more prudent attitude towards opening up its capital markets. \par \par \uc2  "The effects and the degree of damage caused by the Asian financial crisis have far exceeded our predictions," the Financial Morning Post quoted Chen Yuan, governor of the State Development Bank, as saying. \par \par \uc2  "Under an environment of increasing attacks by international capital flows, pre-conditions should be set on opening up of the capital market, the process should have proper order and tempo," he warned. \par \par \uc2  Beijing made the yuan convertible on the current account in 1996, but has held off liberalising the yuan on the capital account. Closed capital markets have spared China the brunt of the financial turmoil that devastated Asia beginning in July last year. \par \par \uc2  China's alarm at the regional financial turmoil and the role of hedge funds and other short-term capital flows have caused it to back away from making the yuan convertible on the capital account anytime soon, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  Chen said Asia's crisis showed that China should adopt stable and healthy macro-economic policies and reduce government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the financial sector. \par \par \uc2  China should boost financial supervision in light of more swift flows of international capital and rising speculative activities, he said. \par \par \uc2  China should also reform its accounting system and boost make economic information more transparent, Chen said. \par \par \uc2  Separately, the Financial News reported authorities in the capital Beijing had stepped up a crackdown on illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealings. \par \par \uc2  Officials of the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and police have raided several black markets in the city and detained a number of illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders, the newspaper said. \par \par \uc2  The black marketeers this week were offering upwards of 8.45 yuan for a dollar. On Friday in Shanghai the yuan ended firmer at 8.2796 to one dollar after closing at 8.2799 close on the previous day. \par \par \uc2  - Beijing Newsroom (86) 10-6532-1921; Fax (86) 10-