\par\par \b \uc2  EU's Brittan calls for Japanese structural reform. \b0\par\par\uc2 368 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1998\par 12:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRUSSELS, June 18 (Reuters) - European Trade Commissioner Sir Leon Brittan on Thursday welcomed U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the yen and urged Japanese authorities to carry out urgent structural reforms. \par \par \uc2  In extracts from a speech to be given to an Australian business group in Brussels, Brittan said a recovery of the Japanese economy and a stronger yen had a crucial part to play in spurring a resumption of growth in Asia, and in easing pressure on other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially the Chinese yuan. \par \par \uc2  "For this to happen, the Japanese authorities now need to embark upon a far-reaching, urgent programme of structural reform, coupled perhaps with a further fiscal boost to stimulate domestic demand," Brittan said. \par \par \uc2  "Under these circumstances, it would be right and proper for the international community to lend Japan its firm support," Brittan said. \par \par \uc2  He said he warmly welcomed an announcement by Japanese Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto on Wednesday that Japan would speed up its fiscal stimulus package, and make further reforms to corporate and personal taxation. \par \par \uc2  He also welcomed U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in support of the yen, which had recently plunged in value. \par \par \uc2  "Individual European countries will no doubt be deciding whether to lend their support," Brittan said. \par \par \uc2  Brittan noted that from next January the EU would have a new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro, with a single \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and would therefore have a much greater role to play in the international monetary system. \par \par \uc2  In future, he said, Europe itself, would be able to take a collective decision to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets as the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  had just been doing. \par \par \uc2  "If on any occasion Europe decides to do so, that will be a decision of global importance, which could have a major impact on the world economy," he said. \par \par \uc2  "So it is essential that the EU should quickly set up the necessary mechanisms to allow rapid decisions to be taken on whether, when, and to what extent the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in situations such as this." ((Brussels Newsroom +32 2 287 6830, fax +32 2 230 5573, brussels.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6i02rua\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
